WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:11.760
Lately, the word BREXIT has become a synonym
for APOCALYPSE for most of the media.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.900
At least, the media outside of UK...

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.730
There are many things to be said about this
but… today, we are going to focus on this.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.680
“Goldman Sachs to move hundreds of staff
out of London”

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.250
And… this is just one example.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.769
At the time we are making this video, 20 banks
have already announced that they will move

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:32.020
part of their staff out of UK.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.410
The most optimistic estimations claim that
the City of London might loose over 30,000

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:37.410
workers…

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.390
And the most pessimistic argue that this could
be as high as 200,000 people.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.860
But wait a minute here!

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.710
Why is this this important?

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.199
Well, if you’ve been following VisualPolitik
for a while, you already know that London

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:51.100
is the global financial capital.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.850
More specifically, that would be the City
of London, also known as the square mile.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.680
The square mile is a city inside of a city
where most of the international banks have

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.210
their headquarters.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.960
Yes, even after Brexit, the chances are London
might keep their sway on the financial markets

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.600
in Asia or even America.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.600
But…

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.600
so far, European business accounts for 35%
of the City’s workload.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.170
And, of course, leaving the European Union
is going to have some consequences.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.600
And now you might wonder…

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.650
Well, where will all these banks go?

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.090
Which city will be the new European financial
capital?

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:31.109
Well… after months of competition, it seems
we do have a winner:

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:37.090
“Banks and companies plan expansion in Frankfurt
after Brexit’

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.909
So far it looks as if Frankfurt is going to
become the new financial powerhouse in Europe,

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:44.140
and perhaps, one of the most important in
the entire world.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.140
But…

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.850
What does Frankfurt have that other cities
like Madrid or Paris don’t?

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.119
Well, today, we are going to answer to this
question, but before we do, let’s take a

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:58.530
look back at the history…

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.570
WHY BANKS ARE LEAVING THE CITY?

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.619
The City of London is an area of about a square
mile (see where the name comes from?) inside

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:06.619
broader London.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:10.110
They have their own laws, their own major
and, even, their own police.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.310
This little space is home for the major international
banks.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.890
300,000 people work in the City, and from
this 300,000 people, 60,000 are European immigrants

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.450
who came into the country thanks to the UK
being a part of the European Union.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.450
And…

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.270
OK, my dear countrymen, I know what you are
thinking: Despite being an EU country, the

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.420
UK never was really inside the free circulation
area.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.580
This means that, theoretically, the British
Government could have closed their borders

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.800
to all those immigrants anytime they wanted.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:46.400
But, in practice, European citizens were getting
in and out of the country as they pleased.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:57.940
Nevertheless, things are going to change.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:06.620
The British Prime Minister, Conservative Theresa
May, holds an extremely weak position.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.600
This is despite the fact that she won the
general election in June.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.120
She has lost a large number of seats in parliament.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.490
In fact, it’s not even clear if she will
finish her term...

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:29.340
And, of course, people from in and out of
her party are pushing her to increase 

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.090
immigration controls.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.150
This would have a big impact in the City of
London, indeed a bigger impact than anywhere

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.400
else in UK.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.570
As we said in another video, part of London’s
success comes from their ability to attract

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.390
talent from all over the world.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.820
But, if the government closes its doors to
foreigners, those banks who hire immigrants

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.400
will have it significantly harder than before.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.960
And make no mistake!

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.010
This is not only due to the BREXIT.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:01.540
In fact, the anti immigrant sentiment in UK
has gone well beyond the borders of European

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.950
Union...

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:13.350
“Theresa May to charge companies £2,000
a year for each non-EU worker they employ.”

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.620
But wait a minute because there’s more!

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.370
As I said, Theresa May and her conservative
party have lost a good share of the seats

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.410
in parliament.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.870
And where did those seats go?

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.370
Well, they went to the Labour party, a party
whose leader, Jeremy Corbin, has declared

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.030
war on the banks.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.970
On his election manifesto, he already proposed
things like this:

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:40.710
“Labour calls for UK financial transaction
tax”

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:45.370
Until now, this tax was one of the biggest
advantages the UK had over the EU.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:50.020
As you might know, Brussels has been years
debating the possibility of having a financial

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.610
transaction tax.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.520
This is the something bankers really don’t
want - no one wants to pay it.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.350
But if UK’s parliament ends up passing such
a law, a lot of companies will be wondering

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.930
if they still want to keep their offices in
London.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.020
In other words…

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.430
even if the Brexit negotiations end up being
great for the UK’s interests, the air is

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.969
still becoming more and more poisonous for
the UK’s financial industry.

00:05:12.969 --> 00:05:18.090
And this explains why so many banks are starting
to think about opening offices out of this

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.090
country.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:22.490
But, now the question is: Why are they choosing
Frankfurt?

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:27.139
Well, let’s have a look at that…

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:30.540
EUROPE’S FINANCIAL CAPITAL?

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.770
On the very day after the Brexit referendum,
the race to take over London as the global

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.110
financial capital started.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:41.189
For example, in Spain, the regional government
of Madrid launched their own advertising campaign

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:45.880
designed to attract bankers.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.699
Even the Finance Minister, LUIS DE GUINDOS
held personal meetings with executives from

00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:52.650
JP Morgan and elsewhere to offer special tax
conditions.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.660
In Paris, Emmanuel Macron has already announced
lower taxes for banks in order to lure them

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:57.880
in from London.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:03.789
But… sorry guys, it’s really hard to beat
the allure of FRANKFURT.

00:06:03.789 --> 00:06:11.340
Standard Chartered Plc, Nomura Holdings Inc.
and Daiwa Securities Group Inc. have picked

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:20.889
Germany’s financial capital for their EU
base to ensure continued access to the single

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:21.889
market.

00:06:21.889 --> 00:06:26.180
Citigroup Inc., Goldman Sachs Group Inc. and
Morgan Stanley are weighing a similar decision

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.180
But…

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.460
what is so special about this city?

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:33.059
Well… first of all, Frankfurt already has
the headquarters of the European Central Bank,

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:35.240
as well as other big banks like Deutsche Bank.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.680
This means, they already have the network
effect we always mention here at VisualPolitik.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.490
They have lots of startups, law firms, consultants,
etc…

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:47.419
Besides, Frankfurt has many business schools
with international prestige, as well as a

00:06:47.419 --> 00:07:09.300
university and the second biggest airport
in Europe.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.789
If this was not enough, 70% of the German
population speak English fluently.

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:17.599
This means those British banks can send their
managers to Frankfurt, hold international

00:07:17.599 --> 00:07:21.560
meetings, and even hire locals without having
to worry about language.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.960
If you think this is not important, good luck
trying to find the same in France, where only

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:30.439
39% of the people speak English.

00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:36.680
Not to mention Spain, where this percentage
is lower at only 25%.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:38.710
But hold on a minute because there’s more!

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:42.969
As I said in a previous video, Germany is
a decentralized country.

00:07:42.969 --> 00:07:47.370
This means regional governments and trade
unions have way more autonomy here than in

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.370
any other country.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:52.740
Thanks to this political architecture, the
region of Hessen, where Frankfurt is located,

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:59.050
can offer things like this:
German labour laws (…) make it difficult

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:04.599
to fire people (…) Frankfurt had initially
proposed a change in labour laws that would

00:08:04.599 --> 00:08:08.449
exempt either bankers, or high earners more
generally, from the rules.

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:10.719
OK, but now let’s think about something
else…

00:08:10.719 --> 00:08:13.379
If you have a bank and you want to set up
your offices somewhere…

00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:15.240
what is the first thing you would need?

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.139
Well, that would be a building!

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:19.729
You need a physical place where you can put
your offices...

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:22.909
Well, in this regard, Frankfurt is better
suited than anywhere else.

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:25.589
You see… after World War II, Frankfurt was
totally destroyed.

00:08:25.589 --> 00:08:28.349
And they had to rebuild it, almost from nothing.

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:32.430
In other German cities, urban laws forbid
the building of skyscrapers in order to maintain

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:34.589
a uniform, more traditional skyline.

00:08:34.589 --> 00:08:43.219
But not in Frankfurt - here you can make buildings
as high as you want.

00:08:43.219 --> 00:08:48.120
This explains why, despite a population smaller
than 1 million inhabitants, Frankfurt looks

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.540
like a big metropolis.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.430
Further, Germany has one of the cheapest real
estate markets in all of Europe.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:57.200
Despite the high salaries, houses, rents and
square meters are half of the price of Paris.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.820
And a fraction of the cost of London…

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:06.649
OK, OK, but now I know what you are thinking:
‘Come on Simon!

00:09:06.649 --> 00:09:08.040
But what about those corporate tax?

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.040
Ay?”.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:10.990
Well, yes, I know…

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.519
Germany has a corporate tax that, in some
cases, reaches to 30%... By comparison the

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:16.519
UK is at 20%.

00:09:16.519 --> 00:09:19.350
And you are right, but the devil really is
in the details here.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.570
Do you remember how I said Germany is highly
decentralized?

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.880
Well… this applies to corporate tax too.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.270
In this country, corporate tax is divided
in two parts: a national and a regional one.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.940
This means the Federal Government gets only
15% of this tax.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:36.470
The rest depends on the region.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:40.610
This means, technically, the State of Hessen,
where Frankfurt is, really has the ability

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.790
to reduce this tax.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.649
And of course, doing this might lure more
and more British bankers.

00:09:45.649 --> 00:09:50.820
And finally, if none of this was enough for
you, here it comes the big finale: the financial

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:55.290
transaction tax - that tax we mentioned before
that might scare away so many bankers.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:59.480
That tax, at least in Europe, has about as
many loopholes as possible.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:03.779
There are so many exceptions on this tax that
even his biggest supporter, the German Finance

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:07.350
Minister, Wolfgang Schaeuble, complained with
these words:

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:10.040
“Even a Swiss cheese needs more than holes.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:14.900
There has to be something in between them,
otherwise it’s just a hole, not a Swiss

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.019
cheese.”

00:10:16.019 --> 00:10:21.310
– Wolfgang Schaeuble
So now we have a question for you: Imagine

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:24.860
you are the manager of one of those London
Banks - what would you do?

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:25.860
Would you remain in London?

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.410
Or, perhaps you would move your offices to
Frankfurt?

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.800
Let us know what you think in the comments
below…

